I think this issue didn't received proper explanation.

If you had something like :
```ts
abstract class Base {
  protected abstract testify:(source: Base) => this;
}

abstract class Derived extends Base {
  protected abstract override testify:(source: Derived) => this;
}
```

This would raise a TS error, as you should be able to substitute any instances of `Base` by one of its children, i.e. :
```ts 
let b: Base;
a.testify(b);
```
should be possible, with `a` being a `Base`, or a `Derived`.
But this is not possible for `Derived` as `testify` only accept `Derived` instances therefore raising an error.

However, you used `this`, which, if I am not mistaken kind of internally transforms the classes into generic classes, something like :
```ts
abstract class Base<T> {
  protected abstract testify:(source: T) => T;
}

abstract class Derived<T> extends Base<T> {
  protected abstract override testify:(source: T) => T;
}
```

In this case, you have `Base = Base<Base>` and `Derived = Derived<Derived>` (extending `Base<Derived>`).
`Base<Derived>` is not assignable to `Base<Base>` for the same reason as previously stated.

In this case, using `this` hides the initial error in the class definition, but would raise error during assignations.

However, as TS uses duck typing, if `Base<T>` and `Derived<T>` have the same interface, then they are assume kind of being "the same". Hence no error when the properties are public.

The issue being that the `protected` attribute seems to kind of wear off the duck typing for implementation reasons.
Protected methods not being a part of the public interface, and being excluded in some type operations.

